1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,960
He began as a machine gunner and became the second most successful sniper of the Wehrmacht.

2
00:00:05,060 --> 00:00:08,180
His name was Josef Allerberger, known as Sepp.

3
00:00:08,420 --> 00:00:15,000
He was born in Austria in 1924, the son of a carpenter, and trained as a carpenter himself.

4
00:00:15,540 --> 00:00:20,200
In 1942 he was called up and trained as a machine gunner.

5
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:27,760
In the summer of 1942 he joined Mountain Infantry Regiment 144 of the 3rd Mountain Division on the Eastern Front.

6
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:29,560
Shortly afterwards he was wounded.

7
00:00:29,580 --> 00:00:37,500
While he was recovering, he picked up a captured Soviet sniper rifle and scored 27 kills with it.

8
00:00:37,580 --> 00:00:42,320
He was then sent to the sniper school on the Seetaler Alpe in Styria.

9
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:47,680
He returned to his division with a Karabiner 98k with a telescopic sight.

10
00:00:47,820 --> 00:00:52,813
Until the end of the war he fought in Ukraine, Romania, Hungary and Slovakia.

11
00:00:52,833 --> 00:00:56,160
He was credited with 257 kills.

12
00:00:56,180 --> 00:00:59,940
Only Matthäus Hetzenauer from the same regiment had more.

13
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:07,540
By his own account he received the Knight's Cross in April 1945, but no official record of it survives.

14
00:01:07,660 --> 00:01:13,380
In May 1945 he laid down his rifle and made his way back to Austria on foot.

15
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:15,920
After that he worked as a carpenter again.

16
00:01:15,940 --> 00:01:20,520
He only spoke about the war decades later, with the author Albrecht Wacker.

17
00:01:20,680 --> 00:01:25,380
Josef Allerberger died in 2010 at the age of 85.
